@* FR-105 to FR-108: a delivery's append-only history, and the two things a viewer may append to it.

   One component for both screens, and it owns its dialog. The dispatch board and the own-deliveries
   screen show the same history and offer the same compose box; what differs is whether the status
   select is drawn, and that is a parameter rather than a role test - the guard inside
   IDeliveryService is the decision, and this only avoids offering an action that could only ever be
   refused (FR-12).

   The dialog lives here rather than in the host because its footer is this panel's: the one submit
   reads both the select and the comment, and a host-owned footer would have to reach into the panel
   to press it. A host renders this component only while a row is chosen; it opens itself after its
   first render, and OnClosed hands the row back so the next open is a fresh panel with an empty box.

   The moves come from Delivery.NextStatuses, which is AD-10's single declaration of the lifecycle.
   Reading it is not writing it: re-listing the transitions here would be exactly the second copy
   that decision exists to prevent. No Delivery instance crosses the boundary - only the static call
   and the DeliveryStatus enum both screens already bind.

   The @@if blocks below are independent rather than if/else chains, and every handler is built in
   code rather than written as a lambda in an attribute: an `else` reads to NFR-14's source scan as
   a rendered English word, and a `=>` inside an attribute closes the tag as far as that scan is
   concerned. *@
@inject IDeliveryService Timeline
@inject IStringLocalizer<UiText> Localizer
@inherits CircuitScopedComponent

<DtDialog @ref="_dialog"
          Title="@Localizer["TimelineTitle"]"
          OnClosed="ClosedAsync">
    <ChildContent>
        @* NFR-3: a refusal resolves through the same catalogue the REST envelope uses, so the dialog
           and the wire cannot disagree about what went wrong. It keeps what the comment box below
           has not claimed: DELIVERY_INVALID_STATUS_TRANSITION and DELIVERY_PROOF_REQUIRED are about
           the delivery rather than about anything typed here, and neither names a field. *@
        <DtFailureBanner Failures="_failures" InlineFields="@InlineFields" />

        <section class="dt-timeline-history" aria-labelledby="@_historyHeadingId">
            <h3 id="@_historyHeadingId" class="dt-type-h3 dt-timeline-heading">@Localizer["TimelineHistory"]</h3>

            @if (_loading)
            {
                <p class="dt-timeline-empty" role="status">@Localizer["Loading"]</p>
            }

            @if (!_loading && _entries.Count == 0)
            {
                <p class="dt-timeline-empty">@Localizer["TimelineEmpty"]</p>
            }

            <ol class="dt-timeline-list">
                @* Oldest first, as the repository ordered them (FR-108). Nothing here re-sorts: a
                   second ordering would be a second answer to "what happened when". *@
                @foreach (var entry in _entries)
                {
                    @* One shared entry per line: the dot's glyph, the rail, the two StatusLabels and
                       the note box are the same on every timeline, and the only things this screen
                       knows that the component cannot are who the actor may be named as and which
                       move is the current one. *@
                    <DtTimelineEntry PreviousStatus="entry.PreviousStatus"
                                     NewStatus="entry.NewStatus"
                                     When="@Occurred(entry)"
                                     Note="@entry.Note"
                                     Current="IsCurrent(entry)">
                        <Actor>
                            @* AD-17: the name when this viewer may be told it, the role badge either
                               way. The service decided which; a null name here means "withheld",
                               never "missing" - a client who may not be told who drove still reads
                               that a driver did.

                               The role is a Badge rather than a StatusLabel: a claim about a person
                               is never dressed in the chrome a delivery's status wears, and every
                               role wears the same badge because roles are not ranked. *@
                            @if (entry.ActorName is not null)
                            {
                                <span class="dt-timeline-actor">@entry.ActorName</span>
                            }
                            @if (entry.ActorRole == UserRole.Admin)
                            {
                                <DtBadge Variant="DtBadgeVariant.Role">@Localizer["RoleAdmin"]</DtBadge>
                            }
                            @if (entry.ActorRole == UserRole.Dispatcher)
                            {
                                <DtBadge Variant="DtBadgeVariant.Role">@Localizer["RoleDispatcher"]</DtBadge>
                            }
                            @if (entry.ActorRole == UserRole.Driver)
                            {
                                <DtBadge Variant="DtBadgeVariant.Role">@Localizer["RoleDriver"]</DtBadge>
                            }
                            @if (entry.ActorRole == UserRole.Client)
                            {
                                <DtBadge Variant="DtBadgeVariant.Role">@Localizer["RoleClient"]</DtBadge>
                            }
                        </Actor>
                    </DtTimelineEntry>
                }
            </ol>
        </section>

        @* FR-107: anyone who can see the delivery may say something about it, including the client
           who may never change its status. FR-106: a status change carries its comment on the same
           entry, so the select and the box are one compose step with one submit. *@
        <section class="dt-timeline-compose" aria-labelledby="@_composeHeadingId">
            <h3 id="@_composeHeadingId" class="dt-type-h3 dt-timeline-heading">@Localizer["TimelineAddHeading"]</h3>

            @* Drawn only when there is a move to offer: a client, or a delivery in a terminal status,
               sees the comment box alone rather than a select whose only option is "keep". *@
            @if (Offered.Count > 0)
            {
                <div class="dt-timeline-field">
                    <label class="form-label" for="@_statusFieldId">@Localizer["TimelineNewStatus"]</label>
                    <DtSelect TValue="DeliveryStatus?"
                              Id="@_statusFieldId"
                              Class="dt-timeline-next-status"
                              Disabled="Busy"
                              AriaDescribedBy="@_moveHintId"
                              @bind-Value="_next">
                        @* The empty value is "keep": a note on its own, which is what null means to
                           the submit below. *@
                        <option value="">@Localizer["TimelineKeep"] @CurrentWord @Localizer["TimelineNoteOnly"]</option>
                        @foreach (var next in Offered)
                        {
                            <option value="@next">@StatusWord(next)</option>
                        }
                    </DtSelect>
                    <div id="@_moveHintId" class="form-text dt-timeline-move-hint">
                        @Localizer["TimelineMoveFrom"] <strong>@CurrentWord</strong> @Localizer["TimelineMoveTo"] @OfferedWords
                    </div>
                </div>
            }

            @* The textarea and its DtFieldError share a parent: that is the shape the theme's
               refused-field outline and the banner's reveal both look for. *@
            <div class="dt-timeline-field">
                <label class="form-label" for="@_noteFieldId">@Localizer["TimelineNote"]</label>
                @* The column's own bound, read from the entity that declares it: an over-long note is
                   stopped in the browser rather than costing a refused round trip. *@
                <textarea id="@_noteFieldId" class="form-control dt-timeline-note-input"
                          placeholder="@Localizer["TimelineNotePlaceholder"]"
                          aria-describedby="@NoteHintReference"
                          disabled="@Busy"
                          maxlength="@TimelineEntry.NoteMaximumLength" @bind="_note"></textarea>
                <DtFieldError Field="Note" Failures="_failures" />
                @* Only beside the select: the hint is about changing the status, so a viewer who is
                   offered no change - a client, a delivery already Delivered - is told nothing. *@
                @if (Offered.Count > 0)
                {
                    <div id="@_noteHintId" class="form-text dt-timeline-note-hint">@Localizer["TimelineNoteHint"]</div>
                }
            </div>
        </section>
    </ChildContent>
    <Actions>
        @* The one submit. Disabled while a write is in flight: the circuit yields at the await, so a
           second click would write the same entry twice. And while the reload that follows one is
           in flight, which no other dialog needs: this panel stays open with its box already
           emptied, and a click landing in that window would post an empty note - bannering a
           refusal over an entry that saved. *@
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary dt-timeline-submit"
                disabled="@(_submitting || _loading)" @onclick="SubmitAsync">
            <Icon Name="IconName.Create" />@Localizer["TimelineSubmit"]
        </button>
        @* Cancel too: closing mid-write disposes the panel before OnChanged fires, and the row
           behind the dialog would keep the status it had before the write landed. *@
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-outline-secondary dt-timeline-cancel"
                disabled="@Busy" @onclick="CancelAsync">
            <Icon Name="IconName.Close" />@Localizer["Cancel"]
        </button>
    </Actions>
</DtDialog>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The boxes this screen renders a <c>DtFieldError</c> for. Stated once and bound by every
    /// banner on the page: the list repeated per banner is exactly the drift FR-83 is broken by.
    /// </summary>
    private const string InlineFields = "Note";

    /// <summary>
    /// Generated rather than fixed: two of these on one screen must not share an id, or a label
    /// would point at the wrong field.
    /// </summary>
    private readonly string _noteFieldId = $"dt-timeline-note-{Guid.NewGuid():N}";

    /// <inheritdoc cref="_noteFieldId" />
    private readonly string _statusFieldId = $"dt-timeline-next-{Guid.NewGuid():N}";

    /// <inheritdoc cref="_noteFieldId" />
    private readonly string _moveHintId = $"dt-timeline-move-hint-{Guid.NewGuid():N}";

    /// <inheritdoc cref="_noteFieldId" />
    private readonly string _noteHintId = $"dt-timeline-note-hint-{Guid.NewGuid():N}";

    /// <inheritdoc cref="_noteFieldId" />
    private readonly string _historyHeadingId = $"dt-timeline-history-{Guid.NewGuid():N}";

    /// <inheritdoc cref="_noteFieldId" />
    private readonly string _composeHeadingId = $"dt-timeline-compose-{Guid.NewGuid():N}";

    private DtDialog? _dialog;
    private IReadOnlyList<TimelineEntryView> _entries = [];
    private IReadOnlyList<ScreenFailure> _failures = [];
    private string? _note;
    private bool _loading = true;

    /// <summary>
    /// The status the select is set to, or null for "keep" - a note on its own. Reset to null
    /// after every write and whenever a reload leaves it naming a move that is no longer legal.
    /// </summary>
    private DeliveryStatus? _next;

    /// <summary>
    /// True while an entry is in flight, which is what disables the submit for as long as it
    /// lasts. The circuit yields at the await, so without it a second click would write the same
    /// entry onto the timeline twice.
    /// </summary>
    private bool _submitting;

    /// <summary>
    /// The delivery whose history is loaded, or zero before the first load. Compared against
    /// <see cref="DeliveryId"/> so a re-render with the same parameters does not refetch.
    /// </summary>
    private int _loaded;

    /// <summary>The index of the entry whose dot is tinted, or -1 when none moved anything.</summary>
    private int _currentEntry = -1;

    /// <summary>
    /// The status the select is computed from. Seeded from <see cref="Status"/> and then advanced by
    /// what the service actually wrote, so the panel offers the moves available from where the
    /// delivery is now rather than from where it was when the dialog opened.
    /// </summary>
    private DeliveryStatus _status;

    /// <summary>The delivery this panel is about.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public int DeliveryId { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Where that delivery is in its lifecycle when the panel opens.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public DeliveryStatus Status { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether to draw the status select. False hides it; it does not authorize anything -
    /// <c>IAccessGuard.RequireAssignedDriver</c> decides, and it runs whether or not this panel was
    /// ever rendered (FR-12).
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool AllowStatusChange { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Raised after a write lands, so the screen behind the dialog can refetch. The status a table
    /// shows would otherwise be the one it was fetched with.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback OnChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Raised once the dialog has closed, however it was dismissed, so the host can drop the row
    /// and with it this panel.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback OnClosed { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The statuses the select offers: what the lifecycle allows from where the delivery is, or
    /// nothing at all when this viewer has no lifecycle action (FR-90).
    /// </summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<DeliveryStatus> Offered =>
        AllowStatusChange ? Delivery.NextStatuses(_status) : [];

    /// <summary>
    /// True while a write or the reload after it is in flight. Every control that could start a
    /// second write, change what the first one sends, or close the panel under it is disabled.
    /// </summary>
    private bool Busy => _submitting || _loading;

    /// <summary>The note hint's id while it renders, so the textarea names it; null otherwise.</summary>
    private string? NoteHintReference => Offered.Count > 0 ? _noteHintId : null;

    /// <summary>The current status in words, for the keep option and the move hint.</summary>
    private string CurrentWord => StatusWord(_status);

    /// <summary>The offered moves in words, in the lifecycle's own order.</summary>
    private string OfferedWords => string.Join(", ", Offered.Select(StatusWord));

    /// <inheritdoc />
    protected override async Task OnParametersSetAsync()
    {
        if (_loaded == DeliveryId)
        {
            return;
        }

        _loaded = DeliveryId;
        _status = Status;
        _failures = [];
        _note = null;
        _next = null;

        await LoadAsync();
    }

    /// <inheritdoc />
    /// <remarks>
    /// The panel opens itself: the host renders it only once a row is chosen, so its first render
    /// is the moment the viewer asked for it.
    /// </remarks>
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (firstRender && _dialog is not null)
        {
            await _dialog.ShowAsync();
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Where the delivery stands according to its own history: the newest entry that moved it, or
    /// <paramref name="fallback"/> when nothing in the history did.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Internal and static so it can be asserted directly: static rendering dispatches no events,
    /// so the reload this feeds is unreachable from a render test.
    /// <para>
    /// Walked from the end because the repository orders entries oldest-first (FR-108), and skipping
    /// the ones with no status is what keeps a note appended after the last move from reading as a
    /// move backwards.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    /// <param name="entries">The delivery's history, oldest first.</param>
    /// <param name="fallback">The status to keep when no entry carries one.</param>
    /// <returns>The status the history ends in.</returns>
    internal static DeliveryStatus LatestStatus(
        IReadOnlyList<TimelineEntryView> entries,
        DeliveryStatus fallback)
    {
        var index = CurrentEntry(entries);

        return index < 0 ? fallback : entries[index].NewStatus!.Value;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The index of the newest entry that moved the delivery - the one whose dot wears the current
    /// status's colour - or -1 when no entry moved anything.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="entries">The delivery's history, oldest first.</param>
    /// <returns>The index, or -1.</returns>
    internal static int CurrentEntry(IReadOnlyList<TimelineEntryView> entries)
    {
        ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(entries);

        for (var index = entries.Count - 1; index >= 0; index--)
        {
            if (entries[index].NewStatus is not null)
            {
                return index;
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether <paramref name="entry"/> is the one whose dot is tinted. By reference rather than by
    /// value: entries are records, and two identical notes are still two lines.
    /// </summary>
    private bool IsCurrent(TimelineEntryView entry) =>
        _currentEntry >= 0 && ReferenceEquals(_entries[_currentEntry], entry);

    /// <summary>
    /// The instant in local time (AD-13: storage is offset zero, and the conversion happens here,
    /// in the presentation layer, and nowhere else).
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Routed through a method rather than formatted in markup: NFR-14's scan strips a Razor
    /// expression only up to its first closing bracket, so a <c>.ToString(...)</c> tail would be
    /// left in the text node and read as a rendered English word.
    /// </remarks>
    private static string Occurred(TimelineEntryView entry) =>
        entry.OccurredAt.ToLocalTime().ToString("g", System.Globalization.CultureInfo.CurrentCulture);

    /// <summary>
    /// A status in words, for places that can hold only text - an &lt;option&gt; and the hint. The
    /// same keys <c>DeliveryStatusLabel</c> reads, so the two cannot name a status differently.
    /// </summary>
    private string StatusWord(DeliveryStatus status) => status switch
    {
        DeliveryStatus.Pending => Localizer["StatusPending"],
        DeliveryStatus.InTransit => Localizer["StatusInTransit"],
        DeliveryStatus.Delivered => Localizer["StatusDelivered"],
        DeliveryStatus.Failed => Localizer["StatusFailed"],
        _ => throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(
            nameof(status),
            status,
            "No status label is defined for this delivery status."),
    };

    private async Task LoadAsync()
    {
        _loading = true;

        StateHasChanged();

        try
        {
            _entries = await Timeline.ListTimelineAsync(DeliveryId, CircuitToken);
            _currentEntry = CurrentEntry(_entries);

            // The select is computed from _status, so a reload has to move it as well as the list.
            // This is the arm that makes the refusal path work: after a 409 caused by somebody
            // else's move, refetching the entries without re-reading where they leave the delivery
            // would redraw the panel around the status it already had, and every retry would be
            // refused for the same reason with nothing on screen having changed.
            _status = LatestStatus(_entries, _status);
        }
        catch (DriveTrackException failure)
        {
            // A refused read is reported rather than thrown: the dialog is open over a screen that
            // is still usable, and taking the whole circuit down for one panel would lose it.
            _failures = FailureKeys.For(failure);
            _entries = [];
            _currentEntry = -1;
        }
        finally
        {
            // In a finally so a refused read leaves the panel in its empty state rather than
            // spinning forever, which reads to a user as a hung dialog.
            _loading = false;
        }

        // A move chosen before the reload may no longer be legal from where the delivery now is;
        // left selected, the next submit would be refused for a choice the select no longer shows.
        _next = KeptChoice(_next, Offered);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The select's choice after a reload: kept while it is still one of the offered moves, null
    /// ("keep") once it is not.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="chosen">What the select held before the reload.</param>
    /// <param name="offered">The moves offered from where the delivery now is.</param>
    /// <returns>The choice, or null.</returns>
    internal static DeliveryStatus? KeptChoice(DeliveryStatus? chosen, IReadOnlyList<DeliveryStatus> offered)
    {
        ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(offered);

        return chosen is { } status && offered.Contains(status) ? status : null;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// What the submit sends: a note on its own for "keep", or a status change carrying the
    /// comment on the same entry (FR-106). Internal and static so the routing can be asserted
    /// directly - static rendering dispatches no events.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="next">The select's choice, null for "keep".</param>
    /// <param name="note">The comment as typed.</param>
    /// <returns>An <see cref="AddDeliveryNoteCommand"/> or a <see cref="ChangeDeliveryStatusCommand"/>.</returns>
    internal static object CommandFor(DeliveryStatus? next, string? note) =>
        next is { } status
            ? new ChangeDeliveryStatusCommand(status, note)
            : new AddDeliveryNoteCommand(note);

    /// <summary>
    /// The footer's one submit: "keep" appends a note, a chosen status moves the delivery and
    /// carries the comment on the same entry (FR-106).
    /// </summary>
    private async Task SubmitAsync()
    {
        if (_submitting)
        {
            return;
        }

        // Set before the await and cleared in the finally: the button is disabled from the moment
        // the first click is handled until the server has answered.
        _submitting = true;

        // Read once: the select may re-render under the await, and the write that is in flight is
        // the one the viewer chose when they pressed.
        var next = _next;

        try
        {
            var command = CommandFor(next, _note);

            if (command is ChangeDeliveryStatusCommand change)
            {
                var entry = await Timeline.ChangeStatusAsync(DeliveryId, change, CircuitToken);

                // From what was written, not from what was asked for: the two agree, and reading
                // the answer is what keeps this panel honest if they ever stop agreeing.
                _status = entry.NewStatus ?? _status;
            }

            if (command is AddDeliveryNoteCommand note)
            {
                await Timeline.AddNoteAsync(DeliveryId, note, CircuitToken);
            }

            _failures = [];
            _note = null;
            _next = null;
        }
        catch (DriveTrackException failure)
        {
            _failures = FailureKeys.For(failure);

            // A refused move is reloaded: the most likely reason a legal-looking move is refused
            // is that somebody else already made one, so the panel's idea of where the parcel is
            // has to be replaced rather than left offering the move that was just rejected. A
            // refused note changed nothing, so the box keeps what was typed and nothing reloads.
            //
            // And the host is told: the reload found a status the row behind the dialog does not
            // show yet, because somebody else moved the delivery.
            if (next is not null)
            {
                await LoadAsync();

                await OnChanged.InvokeAsync();
            }

            return;
        }
        finally
        {
            _submitting = false;
        }

        // Outside the try. The entry has committed, so a failure refreshing the panel afterwards is
        // a failed read, and reporting it as a refused write would tell the viewer their entry was
        // lost when it was not.
        await LoadAsync();

        await OnChanged.InvokeAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>Closes the dialog; <see cref="ClosedAsync"/> does the forgetting.</summary>
    private async Task CancelAsync()
    {
        if (_dialog is not null)
        {
            await _dialog.CloseAsync();
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Every dismissal - Cancel, the head's dismiss, Escape, the backdrop - ends here. The box and
    /// the choice are dropped before the host is told, so even a host that kept this panel alive
    /// would open it next time empty.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task ClosedAsync()
    {
        _note = null;
        _next = null;
        _failures = [];

        await OnClosed.InvokeAsync();
    }
}
